<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rashid Rida</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1865">1865</a>–<a href="/years/1935">1935</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Muhammad Rashid Rida, Muḥammad Rashīd Riḍā</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Muhammad Rashid Rida was an Islamic scholar, reformer, theologian and revivalist, born in al-Qalamoun, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a>, in 1865. He joined his mentor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-abduh">Muhammad Abduh</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> and in 1898 began publishing al-Manar, through which he became one of the most influential <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> jurists of his generation and a leader of the Arab Salafi movement. An early Salafist, he later turned from rationalist leanings to a stricter scripturalist approach, supported the Wahhabi movement and revived the works of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-taymiyya">Ibn Taymiyyah</a>. He opposed secularism, nationalism and European <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperialism">imperialism</a> and championed a pan-Islamist program to re-establish a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliphate</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rashid-rida.jpg" alt="Rashid Rida" width="330" height="381" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MuhammadRashidRida.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Al-Muqtataf moved to Cairo in 1884; Jurji Zaydan founded al-Hilal there in 1892, the year Hind Nawfal launched al-Fatat in Alexandria; and <b>Rashid Rida</b> began al-Manar in 1898.</blockquote><a href="/movement/nahda">Nahda · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>At the end of the 19th century, as European colonial powers dismembered most of the Ottoman Empire, Jamal al-Din al-Afghani (1837–97), Muhammad Abduh (1849–1905) and <b>Rashid Rida</b> (1865–1935) preached Islamic alternatives to that decline.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/islamism">Islamism · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
